 "entity": {
  "name": "Siege of Tenochtitlan",
  "wikidata": "Q593267"
 },
 "title": "Siege of Tenochtitlan",
 "date_label": "26 May - 13 August 1521",
 "year": 1520,
 "intro": "In 1521 Hernan Cortes and his native allies besieged Tenochtitlan, the capital of the Mexica, an island\n            city in Lake Texcoco joined to the shore by three causeways. After the disaster of the Noche Triste, Cortes\n            rebuilt his force around Tlaxcala, won over lakeshore peoples, and had thirteen brigantines built to\n            contest the water. The siege opened in late May with three land divisions at Tacuba, Coyoacan and\n            Iztapalapa. It lasted roughly eighty days, through desperate fights on the causeways, a near disaster\n            on St John's day, famine and smallpox in the city, and a final push to Tlatelolco. It ended with the\n            capture of Cuauhtemoc on 13 August.",
 "outcome": "Spanish and Tlaxcalan victory: Tenochtitlan fell, the Aztec Empire ended and New Spain began.",
 "uncertainty": "Causeway routes, the island outline, the Chapultepec aqueduct and all intermediate positions of\n            the three divisions are reconstructed from the narrative sources and marked conjectural; the sources give no\n            exact days for most phases. Strengths are contested: allied totals range from 50,000 to 200,000, and\n            the death toll from 100,000 to 240,000. The sources are largely Spanish, with Cortes and Diaz at their head.",
 "sources": [
